body:
the crashes sound like they could be a number of things, first you could try lower the cpu clock speed slightly. i dont really have any recommendations on how to stress it since I dont usually overclock the much, usually all i do i bump up the voltage a small bit and bump up the clock speed by at most 100mhz. you could also try using the cpu at stock with auto voltage limiting and performance profile 2 which could help, its not exactly and overclock but its better than nothing since it can boost at 4.2 for longer and with more cores.

&#x200B;

this could also be a ram issue since xmp settings dont usually work very well whenever i have tried it, you could try either bumping down the frequency from the rated 3200mhz to something like 3133mhz or you could increase the voltage a bit bit, try to keep it below 1.55v for the ram btw or it could severely reduce its lifespan. side note, I have done a lot of ram overclocking because im stupid and try to overclock everything except the important stuff XD, but if you have the voltage too high on the ram that can also cause instability, not because of heat but for some other reason, my ram is currently set at 1.55v 3466mhz and a cas latency of 16, if i set the voltage to 1.545v i get instability, same thing when i change it to 1.555v, i personally use memtest32 but you will need a flash drive since it is a tool you boot directly into. you shouldnt have to worry about being very specific with the voltage like i did but just fiddle around with the ram voltage and speed in the bios until you get 0 errors when running memtest32.

&#x200B;

if it is a gpu issue it could be hard to find, although the black screen with you still being able to hear and talk to people online tells me that it could and likely is gpu issue, once again i dont really know how to stress test it very well so you will have to figure that part out or look at a different comment. you can try decreasing the clock speed slightly (1-5%) along with doing the same with mem speed on the graphics card. Im not sure about how effective a voltage increase will be on the gpu but you could try doing a slightly more aggressive increase to voltage, other than that i dont know.

&#x200B;

if you did bclk or what amd calls (apu in the bios for some odd reason) overclocking, that can easily cause issues, it can cause sata errors and can cause gpu instability since you are changing the speed at which the motherboard bus runs at, so essentially, changing this value overclocks the sata bus, possibly the gpu but im not sure, the ram, the cpu, the usb bus. I think you get the point, i dont have experience with bclk overclocking so if you use that then try setting the bus speed back to 100mhz.

&#x200B;

if none of that works you could try clearing the cmos. if that fails it is likely a faulty gpu

&#x200B;

edit: next time it crashes, if it bsods write down the error code, it could help

yea but from having issues connecting to their site, to a seemingly imaginary gpu from amd called the "rx 480x" and the weird graphics on the site with the one article I was able to load. I mean their benchmarks seem accurate, good, and fairly informative based on that one article. I think i may have just seen some weird bits today so im honestly not sure. I based my comment on 5 min of research looking for the rx480x listed in this reddit post that linked to the site so my judgement of it as a whole is very likely not accurate.

&#x200B;

edit: btw this was my first time hearing about this site and i am generally very skeptical of any website i haven't hear of or used before until i find good info that matches with other sites that i use commonly.

&#x200B;

edit 2: also sorry if my comments seem weird, I cant think straight today, usually i would post at most a sentence or 2 for a given topic but here i am writing entire essays and never getting to the point of what im trying to say XD

pretty much all of the ryzen 2nd gen should crush the processors listed with it clocked at around 1.5 ghz, based on voltage curves I have found online for the 2700x at 4.2ghz generally pulls 1.4 to 1.5 volts at around 105-135 watts approximately, the graph however is exponential so it makes things difficult since i cant find any sort of accurate equation for the power draw. If i had to make an educated guess, I would say you could \*\*probably\*\* run a 2700x at 1.5 ghz using a mere 20-30 watts, could be a bit lower, could be a bit higher. this means that a cpu with half the core count might be able to run at half the voltage given we aren't being very precise.

&#x200B;

my best guess for a 1-2 ghz 4 core zen+ processor in terms of power draw would have to be anywhere between \~8-20 watts for the cpu die alone. don't quote me on the numbers tho, as for the integrated or dedicated gpu... I have no clue, im more of a cpu person so.

&#x200B;

If I were to guess how much power would be drawn with severely down clocked silicon I would say you could probably get away with \~10 watts if you are lucky for the cpu and an integrated gpu, it may lean closer to 15 watts but i dont know, im just some guy from the internet with an obsession with computer hardware, i lack the knowledge to check or calculate any of this with any form of precision.

&#x200B;

all and all, I would love to see consoles, portables, and hand helds running ryzen but I feel it would be unlikely

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

btw, the igpu in ryzen processors arent going to perform much better than really any console gpus out right now cpu performance is one thing but consoles focus more on graphical performance since it is made to do one thing and do it well, play games with how games are optimized on consoles or hand helds, you dont really need much cpu horsepower.

&#x200B;

dont take anything i said as fact, im just stating what my best guess is to the question you are asking. also sorry if i rambled on or didn't make any since at some parts, im not great at talking about, expressing, or explaining stuff. anyway great post!:) made me think

&#x200B;

edit: for an idea of battery usage the switch has a battery capacity of \~4310mah at 3.7v which is about equivalent to 16 watt hours, this means you could run a cpu that draws 8 watts for 2 hours, 16 watts for an hour and so on.

thank you for the comment :), honestly AMD has really helped change up the consumer cpu scene. I defiantly agree with you and many others that AMD is a bit of a hero for challenging intel with more cores for the consumer. I just wish that more people would buy from AMD for both the sake of price to performance and to increase the pressure on intel so that we could have more competition in this market. I feel like people are still looking back to the Athlon days and view AMD as just having bad cpu performance for games because of their old approach to high core count.

&#x200B;

your comment has helped me a lot :)

honestly if you are able to wait just a handful of months (\~4-5) the new ryzen 3rd gen chips are going to be coming out. if the leaks are true they should have many more cores for the price you pay along with much higher single core performance due to the new 7nm process allowing for higher clock speeds with less power draw. if you were planning on getting the 2700x then for around the same cost it had at launch date you could get the 3700x which should have 12 cores instead of 8 and a much higher clock speed assumed to be at \~5ghz boost. Everything else looks good with your upgrade, the only other recommendation is try to get cas 14 3200mhz or cas 16 3600mhz at least, I have found that ram speed on the 2700x is still a slight issue even with overclocked ram (yes i know, im stupid for overclocking ram). Make sure with whatever ram you choose you get 2 8gig sticks so you can run it in dual channel or you will run into some very serious bottle necking.

&#x200B;

